For our last speaker tonight is Derek Shi, a software engineer at the Volian Group in Berkeley.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:30.800
He graduated this year from Carnegie Mellon Masters in Computer Science, where he researched multi-agent LLM systems and AI safety.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:38.160
Um co-advised by Vincent Konitzer at CMU and Ji Jing Jin at the University of Toronto.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:45.040
He is called co-author of When Agents Lie, Premeditation, Persistent and Exploitation in Repeated Games.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.280
Please welcome Jerek.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.000
Wait, thank you, Camille, for the intro.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.880
And yeah, without further ado, let's get started.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.680
Hopefully, the hackathon is going well for everyone.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.720
I think this is like what the beginning of the hackathon.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.360
So yeah, hope everyone is doing well.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:15.440
And yeah, my goal for this talk is just to give like a bit more insight or maybe some more ideas that people can work on based on like just some of like the different research themes that I have been working on.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.600
So yeah, as Camilla mentioned, I study at Carnegie Mellon.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.360
I focused on computer science.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:22.640
They were my masters.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:30.160
My research was mostly focused on multi-agent LLM systems and AI safety, where I was advised by Vincent Kenitzer and Chi Jing.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:36.240
And currently I work as a software engineer in the Bay, and I still work remotely with the Genesis Lab.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:44.400
So going back to the overall context on why we're working on collusion, why we're like dealing with these sprints, things like that.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.680
We need to think back on like what happened with like AI safety in the past few years.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:56.080
So when we first thought about AI safety, we've been focusing a lot on single agent capability and safety.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:04.600
When you feed an LLM some sort of information, we want to make sure that the output is safe, it is not hallucinating, it doesn't give harmful information, etc.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:04.920
etc.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:17.080
But as everyone is starting to adopt these agents for themselves, there is a much bigger question we need to think about, which is this idea of a large group of LLM agents.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:29.080
Because then there are a lot of new safety risks that we have never thought about before that needs to now be addressed, which is kind of why we're now thinking about like collusion in such a large scale.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:38.600
We're reaching a point where a lot of these different actions, a lot of these like regularly actions done by human are now done by these AI agents.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:47.320
So we now have AI agents that can negotiate with each other, they can set different prices in the markets, they can coordinate different tasks.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:58.120
So that because of that, there are a lot more things we need to think about since these agents have essentially kind of taken over this world.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:08.600
There has been a lot, and I mean like a lot of work that has shown that LLMs currently kind of lie, they currently misrepresent all of its different actions.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.040
The earliest one goes all the way back to 2023.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:27.560
This author showed that if you feed GPT 4.0 some insider information regarding trading in the market, and then a manager asks it like what it knows about, it will purposely show in its reasoning that it hides this insider information.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:33.400
And that behavior or similar behaviors has then appeared many times in other different papers.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:40.920
They've shown that these LLM agents are capable of scheming, these agents are capable of colluding, algorithmic colluding, in fact.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:53.280
And I've kind of shown that these agents can then directly lie or break your promises in public to other people, other agents when negotiating in these different scenarios.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:04:06.560
So kind of going over the flow of what my research themes kind of look like, they all fall over this similar umbrella on how can we trust these LLM agents?

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:15.440
Because it's kind of hard to define trust or deception, and you can't just say whether an agent lie or doesn't lie.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:28.480
There is just a lot more nuance, and my goal is to kind of open up this new ones, dive deeper into these differences and see exactly how we can like analyze these deceptive behaviors.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:41.200
So just starting with one of my papers, essentially the goal here is to show that deception isn't as easily defined as what people kind of thrown around.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:52.400
So if we look into past few years' research, everyone has been saying that oh, LLMs are becoming deceptive, they can harm other people, they can lie to each other.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.440
People have been just been throwing these words around.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:08.160
But it's hard to say exactly what do you mean by an agent trying to lie or deceive another agent or another person because let's think about hallucination, for example.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:19.200
If we define deception purely as giving someone wrong information or convincing someone on a false fact, that's exactly what hallucination is.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.680
A hallucinated citation, for example.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:37.000
But if you kind of think about this from a much further perspective, that doesn't really feel like deception because a hallucinated citation feels more as a simple error, and LLM didn't intentionally feel like it's lying to achieve some other goal.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:42.760
So in that case, it's really hard to tie this concept of hallucination with deception.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:48.040
If we think about this a little further, what if an agent is told to like lie on command?

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:56.200
Let's say you're playing a werewolf game with a bunch of LLM agents, and some of these agents are told that they are allowed to lie to deceive other players.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:07.000
This then becomes a lot more nuanced because in these games, well, because these LLMs are prompted to then lie and deceive other agents, therefore they're considered deceptive.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:12.600
But again, is it really deception if they are essentially following instructions?

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:18.680
So in this paper, I claim that deception has to hit a few different categories.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:35.480
In addition for purely saying that something is false, it also needs to hit the category that the model should have known better, aka, it still has the ability to produce a correct answer, and such a deceptive behavior has to serve some specific goal.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:42.600
So, in order to kind of like go a bit more technical, a bit more in depth, these are kind of the three tiers I am proposing.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:49.320
Whenever we think about these different behaviors, we must first assume, we must first decide whether it is false or not.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:53.400
If it is false, that is immediately just misrepresentation.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:07:00.600
And this makes classifying things extremely easy because most of these different papers fall into misrepresentation.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:15.600
I think this second tier, this tier one deception, is the hardest part to understand, but it's also the hardest part to classify, and is what a lot of the current research is going wrong, is this idea of divergence and functional intent.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:22.000
Divergence meaning it is still able to produce actions that are both right and wrong.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:31.920
And functional intent means that when it produces such a false information, it produces such an information because of some sort of underlying goal.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:46.400
And finally, we didn't add like a last tier where if such a behavior then manipulates another person's choice, conditioned on the fact that it's already on this tier one deception, then we call it manipulation.

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:53.520
So, as a quick example, mask and insider trading are two pretty well known benchmarks.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.520
I highly recommend people to take a look.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:08.160
Mask essentially shows that if you continuously pressure an LLM into like answering a question, you can continue pressuring it to give the wrong answer or the answer on the different side.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:11.920
So the question here is that does this count as deception?

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:22.800
Well, since the prompt doesn't say like give the incorrect answer or like order wrong answer, and since it starts off giving the right answer, that means that it does in fact hit divergence.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:30.960
But this LLM, because it was pressured to say the opposite answer, is pressure to say the wrong answer, it doesn't have a purpose.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:38.000
It didn't have a real reason to kind of then give the the wrong answer, essentially.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:45.040
So this is what this paper was missing and why I would classify as purely as a T0 kind of paper.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:50.720
T0 doesn't mean bad, it just means that it only falls under the misrepresentation tier.

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:57.920
Going back to insider training paper, insider training paper where an agent can trade on some insider tip.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:59.280
Now it has a purpose.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:01.800
It wants to hide this missing information.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:03.960
So it does have functional intent.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:16.600
But it was never shown in a paper that it has the possibility or it can choose that it could actually act honestly without all of this different setup, which is why it doesn't hit this divergence criterion.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:25.320
So these are just a few examples on what it really takes to show that an LLM is pretty much deceptive.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:41.400
So just some overall takeaways, some overall thoughts from this paper from what I have gotten is that a lot of these papers, 92 of these different papers have shown or has claimed in some way, in some sentence, that LLMs are becoming deceptive.

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:44.360
They are they are showing deceptive behavior.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:47.480
I claim that that really isn't true.

00:09:47.480 --> 00:10:17.080
And what this means is that whenever people or I guess like people in Hackathon or in general are thinking about like just collusion or all of these different misrepresentative behaviors in general, think about exactly why or how exactly is a kind of manipulating, how exactly is it colluding, what evidence is it need to show that it is colluding, or in general, how to show that something is truly a deceptive behavior.

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:27.800
Another flavor of my work that essentially goes into one of these different categories, one of these different deceptive behaviors, which I focus on promise breaking.

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:45.600
So the main idea here is that even breaking a promise itself is a bit nuanced because there are a lot of scenarios where an LLM might be able to plan something beforehand or announce something beforehand and then actually do the action.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:50.160
There are a lot of combinations that you can think about under these different scenarios.

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:53.840
So one example I like to bring up is Diner's Dilemma.

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:57.440
It is a pretty famously known game theoretic setting.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:00.800
You have five different diners, they want to split the bill evening.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:05.040
Each of them have a choice to choose a cheap versus expensive dish.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:11.840
They all benefit if they choose the cheap dish, but then in the Nash Equilibrium, it benefits when one of them chooses an expensive dish.

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:14.000
So they all choose the expensive dish.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:19.680
So imagine prisoner's dilemma, but in a multi-agent setting.

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:28.000
Depending on what you do, you can first tell other agents that you'll choose the cheap dish, and then in the end, you secretly choose the expensive dish.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:31.280
This is something that I would call commitment breaking.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:42.480
But if you essentially plan out first that you're gonna choose the expensive dish, you then tell other people that it's cheap, and then finally you order the expensive dish.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:45.760
This then goes into premeditated deception.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:50.960
And then this idea kind of follows when you go under these different other different categories.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:12:03.600
Essentially, if you maybe initially like planned out that you're gonna be a good person, choose a cheap dish, but essentially, then in the end, switch your role and choose the expensive dish, that goes into impulsive deviation.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:08.160
So the idea here is that then there are all these different kinds of deviations.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:15.440
Technically, they all count as deception in some sort of way because you're going against what you said or what you planned earlier.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:21.080
But there are many of these different categories that have to be considered.

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:24.360
This is kind of the overall experimental setup.

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:27.640
Essentially, we focus on two different settings.

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:40.600
In one of these different settings, it is a pretty simple example where every single one of these agents can choose what they want to say, and then finally, based on what everyone says, choose what action they actually want to do.

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:43.480
This is kind of like the baseline scenario.

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:51.320
We then go into a much more complicated scenario where these agents can first plan and then they announce and then they do their private actions.

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:53.000
So we add this planning step.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:13:03.080
And we can make this even more complicated if we repeat these games over several different rounds, then remembering what happened in these past different rounds.

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:15.400
Perhaps one of the more I want to say interesting, it is an interesting finding, but a significant finding is that promise breaking doesn't depend either directly on the model or on the game.

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:21.240
There has been a lot of terms thrown a while around where people say that maybe one LLM is more deceptive than the other.

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:24.600
Maybe one game produces like worse behaviors than the other.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:27.720
What I show is that it simply isn't true.

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:33.800
You cannot find a single model that purely dominates another model or game that purely dominates another game.

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:44.360
These LLMs are extremely finicky in the fact that depending on how you put these models and games together, you can get extremely different outcomes.

00:13:56.200 --> 00:14:05.560
And that oftentimes they don't really think about the fact that they're breaking promises, but more so they're just optimizing the solution.

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:20.400
And finally, another thing I want to show kind of ties to the fact in finding one where again these LLMs are pretty interesting to work with, and the fact that these different models can have extremely different behaviors when you put them in a very same setting.

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:26.880
You can have a model where over time they all converge to often going saying that they're gonna do the cheap dish.

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:31.200
You can go into scenarios where all agents will always go to and do the expensive dish.

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:36.000
You can have cases where it goes into round robin where it's kind of like a tit tat method.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:49.520
Sometimes they say cheap, sometimes they say expensive, and then go back and forth, which just makes which just shows that it's shows a lot of interesting scenarios can happen when you mix these different models and games together.

00:14:49.520 --> 00:15:02.000
One final thing, this work is still kind of a work in progress, but this is kind of an idea that I like to call emergent deception, and I think this theme is slowly becoming much more relevant.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:07.440
I think starting this year due to the increasing capabilities of LLMs.

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:21.200
So the main here, the main question I want to show is kind of addressing the fact in the first part where a lot of people have shown that LLAs are deceptive purely because you tell them in prompt that they can lie or manipulate other people.

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:22.720
But what if you don't do that?

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:32.400
Is it possible to achieve these same outcomes, achieve these same behaviors purely due to environmental settings that are not easily as measurable in game theoretic setting?

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:38.320
So this is just a toy simulation toy setting that I have been playing with.

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:44.320
You have four different agents in a resource gathering game, you have gold, water, food, yada yada yada.

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:45.280
You can make trades.

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:51.920
Your goal is to make the most gold, but you want to keep this entire civilization alive with food and water.

00:15:51.880 --> 00:16:01.080
And the idea here is that over time, when you play this game, there is gonna be less food and water for everyone, but there's gonna be more and more gold.

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:12.840
And because these agents in their prompt they like to have gold more than food or water, what agents will they plan on doing because of these environmental pressures?

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:25.160
And the main thing I find is that these agents, okay, first of all, even without telling them at all that they should lie or they should like kind of like the main agents.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:31.160
The biggest finding I found that they in fact are able to commit such actions without these different prompts.

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:39.000
But in most of these different scenarios, the way they deceive other agents falls into fact of selective disclosure.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:43.480
Essentially, they might say they may try to hide some information that they found.

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:51.320
Maybe they found like extra gold, they found extra food or water in a certain case, but they won't share it with the other agents just to hide it for themselves.

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:56.040
Sometimes they just act completely silent so they can act on this information themselves.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:17:01.960
These are some of the key, I guess, like emergent deceptive properties that I have been seeing.

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:08.840
And all of this has emerged without telling them that okay, they should or they are allowed to commit these different actions.

00:17:08.840 --> 00:17:15.960
They explore it, they realized that these actions are in fact the most beneficial for themselves.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:35.480
So one idea is essentially tying back to this insider trading information, right?

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:44.960
The idea is that take all of these different games or take all of these different LM agents, put them maybe in like some actual firm and then have some monitor on them.

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:47.360
Can you find some exact point?

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:56.080
Or can you find under what pressure do these agents kind of either go into silence or to kind of like hide hidden information, things like that?

00:17:56.080 --> 00:18:01.120
And this kind of goes into this idea of like maybe there's some like scaling off or deception.

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:14.160
Another thing that I have been thinking about, which I think ties extremely closely to this hackathon, which I'll talk about later, are how these toy evaluations kind of connect to these real worlds.

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:29.200
And I think this is a big gap because if you look at some of the more recent papers, you realize that all of them go into an extremely similar setting of they kind of propose this very specific outcome.

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:37.920
They kind of propose this very specific game, such as a werewolf game, such as a werewolf game with like some extra scenarios, without ever tying it to real world cases.

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:41.920
And I feel like this is an extremely big gap that needs to be solved.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:47.760
Some other things that I've been thinking about is essentially silence as a deceptive tactic in general.

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:54.080
Obviously, in the case I just showed, when you're silent, misholding information, it kind of harms everyone but benefits yourself.

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:59.520
But there's obviously certain cases because of like privacy issues that you do want to stay silent.

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:02.640
So are there ways to kind of then increase this gap?

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:10.880
And finally, I think the one that I am personally most interested in is just scaling all of these different simulations.

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:18.880
What happens when you maybe have a hundred different agents under all of these different roles, maybe in like a bigger village civilization?

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:29.920
What happens when you have one agent or one event that causes some sort of incident and how to kind of like cascades over this entire civilization?

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:40.200
And finally, I guess, like as I kind of mentioned, by from these future directions, this is how I personally feel like you could stand out in this hackathon.

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:43.400
Um, every single talker is gonna have their own opinions.

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:48.280
Some of the other talkers focus more on collusion, scenographic collusion.

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:53.560
So they definitely have their like own takes, but this is personally just my opinions.

00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:55.400
I feel like there are two main ways.

00:19:55.400 --> 00:20:01.880
I think there are two main ideas that you can kind of pursue under these different like themes that were given.

00:20:01.880 --> 00:20:07.480
The first idea is kind of think about collusion in a more creative way.

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:18.440
I think there are some most of collusion has been look into it with the very same lens on how these different collusive behaviors can kind of emerge.

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:28.360
My question is can you think of some different way, some different way that collision can emerge, or some way to detect collusion that no one has ever thought of?

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:34.440
And as you slowly do the literature research, you'll realize that a lot of them kind of fall into these different buckets.

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:39.480
So if you can come up with something that's out of these different buckets, that would in fact stand out.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:50.040
That could be the environment itself, it could be talking like what agent that you're playing with, or all of these different interactions or ways that these agents can share information to each other.

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:57.880
The second way is the second one is kind of what I mentioned a bit earlier, connecting these simulations to real world risk.

00:20:57.880 --> 00:21:03.480
How do you and how do you convince people that okay, these simulations are important?

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:08.920
These simulations can happen on like some one-on-one correspondence with what might happen in the real world.

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:33.120
And that just comes with thinking about like maybe either scaling up experiments, grounding scenarios and what might have happened in a real world, and something that might be interesting to include is to maybe tell these different firms like maybe like a very simple explanation on just one step or just one agent can like mitigate collusion or stop like these deceptive behaviors from happening.

